3.1 Spectral decomposition. By [F7] and [F8] the nonzero eigenvalues of $S$ are positive real numbers of finite multiplicity with no accumulation point except $0$; enumerate the distinct eigenvalues in decreasing order as $
u_1>
u_2>\cdots>0$ when the set is infinite (with $
u_k\downarrow0$), and let $P_k$ be the orthogonal projection onto the eigenspace $E_{
u_k}$. Since $S$ is injective, [F7] gives $L^2_0(\Omega)=\overline{\operatorname{span}} igcup_kE_{
u_k}$ with orthogonal summands, so for every $f\in L^2_0(\Omega)$ the net of partial sums $f_n:=\sum_{k\le n}P_kf$ converges to $f$ in $L^2$ and $\|f\|_{L^2}^2=\sum_k\|P_kf\|_{L^2}^2$. Every eigenspace lies in $V$, because $Sg\in V$ and $g=
u^{-1}Sg$ for an eigenvector. Finally, for $g\in E_{
u_k}$ and every $v\in V$ one has $a(Sg,v)=(g,v)_{L^2}$ by definition of $S$, that is $a(g,v)=
u_k^{-1}(g,v)_{L^2}$. [F7, F8, step 2.1, algebra]

6.1 Rayleigh characterisation. Put $\mu_k:=
u_k^{-1}$, so that by [F8] $\mu_1=
u_1^{-1}=1/\|S\|$ is the smallest of the $\mu_k$ and $\mu_k>0$. For $f\in V\setminus\{0\}$ step 5.1 and $\|f\|_{L^2}^2=\sum_k\|P_kf\|_{L^2}^2$ give $$ rac{a(f,f)}{\|f\|_{L^2}^2}= rac{\sum_k\mu_k\|P_kf\|_{L^2}^2}{\sum_k\|P_kf\|_{L^2}^2}\ \ge\ \mu_1,$$ with equality precisely when $P_kf=0$ for every $k$ with $\mu_k>\mu_1$, that is $f\in E_{
u_1}$. Hence $\mu_1>0$ is attained and the minimisers are exactly the nonzero elements of $E_{
u_1}$. Moreover $f\in E_{
u_1}\setminus\{0\}$ satisfies $a(f,v)=\mu_1(f,v)_{L^2}$ for all $v\in V$ by step 3.1; conversely, if $0
e f\in V$ satisfies $a(f,v)=\mu_1(f,v)_{L^2}$ for all $v\in V$, then the same expansion gives $\sum_k(\mu_k-\mu_1)\|P_kf\|_{L^2}^2=0$ with all coefficients nonnegative, so $P_kf=0$ whenever $\mu_k>\mu_1$ and $f\in E_{
u_1}$. Thus the eigenspace $\{u\in V:a(u,v)=\mu_1(u,v)_{L^2}\  orall v\in V\}$ equals $E_{
u_1}$, and no mean-zero weak Neumann eigenvalue $\lambda\in(0,\mu_1)$ exists, since it would give the same identity with a nonnegative combination vanishing. [F8, step 3.1, step 5.1, algebra]
